  run_ids: ["f3b817d9-1f0f-45ae-9667-8938f7267e26"] -> ["6fb8f7c7-c58b-4e18-8dfd-cdc47f31e799"]
  explanation: "byteorder-lite 0.1.0: fixed-width unsigned and signed integer writes, plus BE/LE write_f32/write_f64, write their exact bit representations in the selected endian order and preserve trailing bytes when the output is long enough; shorter buffers panic before writing. Float proofs quantify over every 32/64-bit representation, including NaN payloads and signed zeros, under explicit bit-pattern and std to_bits models. No floating arithmetic is modeled. Signed write_i24 and write_int additionally serialize the low 24 or 8*nbytes bits for every signed input value, preserving suffix bytes; no signed representability condition is required. Invalid byte counts or short buffers panic as specified. Signed read_int/read_i24 additionally return the exact two-complement prefix decode at the selected width; all invalid counts and short inputs assert before sign extension. LittleEndian from_slice_u16/u32/u64/u128 and from_slice_i16/i32/i64/i128 additionally leave every slice unchanged on the verified little-endian target, including empty slices and all element values. The two handwritten Default methods always panic. The four variable-width unsigned writers additionally have exact byte-count/space guards, selected-byte output on success and assertion failure otherwise. Unsigned 24-bit readers/writers additionally have exact 3-byte decoding/serialization, a 24-bit value domain and complete short-buffer/value-overflow failures. Four unsigned variable-width readers decode the exact selected prefix under their complete byte-count/input-length guard, and assert otherwise." -> "byteorder-lite 0.1.0: fixed-width unsigned and signed integer writes, plus BE/LE write_f32/write_f64, write their exact bit representations in the selected endian order and preserve trailing bytes when the output is long enough; shorter buffers panic before writing. Float proofs quantify over every 32/64-bit representation, including NaN payloads and signed zeros, under explicit bit-pattern and std to_bits models. No floating arithmetic is modeled. Signed write_i24 and write_int additionally serialize the low 24 or 8*nbytes bits for every signed input value, preserving suffix bytes; no signed representability condition is required. Invalid byte counts or short buffers panic as specified. Signed read_int/read_i24 additionally return the exact two-complement prefix decode at the selected width; all invalid counts and short inputs assert before sign extension. LittleEndian from_slice_u16/u32/u64/u128 and from_slice_i16/i32/i64/i128 additionally leave every slice unchanged on the verified little-endian target, including empty slices and all element values. Native LittleEndian from_slice_f32/f64 also preserve every memory bit under the existing representations, including NaN payloads and signed zeros; this is not a Rust floating PartialEq claim. The two handwritten Default methods always panic. The four variable-width unsigned writers additionally have exact byte-count/space guards, selected-byte output on success and assertion failure otherwise. Unsigned 24-bit readers/writers additionally have exact 3-byte decoding/serialization, a 24-bit value domain and complete short-buffer/value-overflow failures. Four unsigned variable-width readers decode the exact selected prefix under their complete byte-count/input-length guard, and assert otherwise."
  trusted_assumptions: "F32/F64 are explicit 32/64-bit logical patterns; two executable std to_bits models preserve those patterns as unsigned words, with checked Lean value-preservation lemmas. Guards require original writer bodies and allow no other float operation. Kani checks std bit identity for every pattern and actual released writers with 6/10-byte buffers. Connecting these Rust checks to the Lean representation is reviewed, not mechanized; Rust nightlies differ. Official translation/standard integer and slice models, Lean, and Kani/CBMC are trusted. The eight native conversion bodies make no external calls after official target-branch translation. Nine unused declarations remain in the unchanged, unimported external template; the provider supplies zero definitions and no semantics are assumed for those unused symbols. Guards require exactly the original identity bodies and zero external use. Mapped audits permit only propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound." -> "F32/F64 are explicit 32/64-bit logical patterns; two executable std to_bits models preserve those patterns as unsigned words, with checked Lean value-preservation lemmas. Guards require original writer bodies and allow no other float operation. Kani checks std bit identity for every pattern and actual released writers with 6/10-byte buffers. Connecting these Rust checks to the Lean representation is reviewed, not mechanized; Rust nightlies differ. Official translation/standard integer and slice models, Lean, and Kani/CBMC are trusted. The ten native conversion bodies make no external calls after official target-branch translation. Thirteen native-path declarations remain in the unchanged, unimported external template. The native provider imports the existing bit-pattern representation module and supplies zero new definitions or operation models; native bodies invoke no external operation. Existing to_bits models remain used by the earlier float writers only. Guards require exactly the original identity bodies and zero external use. Mapped audits permit only propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound."
  evidence_url: "https://github.com/nyuichi/rust-crate-proofs/tree/500f8c504e9b5cccb7c0ad3791eea77c98707baf" -> "https://github.com/nyuichi/rust-crate-proofs/tree/46f0bb3d48a1142d193022f16e8b3eb7d662e44f"
  limitations: "This covers 30 selected writer methods, ten scoped integer readers, eight native-target identity conversions, and two handwritten Default methods, not all public APIs. Fixed-width 16/32/64/128-bit reads, remaining signed 128-bit variable-width wrappers, other bulk and in-place conversions, I/O extensions, other targets and release extraction remain unproved. The float result establishes bit serialization only: floating arithmetic, comparisons, rounding, numeric accuracy, NaN arithmetic and signaling behavior are not modeled. Kani public-writer buffer lengths are fixed at 6 and 10; arbitrary valid slice lengths are quantified separately by the Lean theorems. The write_int documentation says nonrepresentable values panic, while original-body theorems establish truncation for all i64 inputs when count/space guards hold; 176 finite native observations corroborate selected boundaries. This is a documentation/behavior mismatch, not a memory-safety finding. write_i24 documents only short-buffer panic and is not classified as a documentation problem. Other signed reads remain unproved. Opposite-endian conversion methods and other target configurations are not covered by the identity results. Panic messages and unwinding side effects are not specified." -> "This covers 30 selected writer methods, ten scoped integer readers, ten native-target identity conversions, and two handwritten Default methods, not all public APIs. Fixed-width 16/32/64/128-bit reads, remaining signed 128-bit variable-width wrappers, other bulk and in-place conversions, I/O extensions, other targets and release extraction remain unproved. The float result establishes bit serialization only: floating arithmetic, comparisons, rounding, numeric accuracy, NaN arithmetic and signaling behavior are not modeled. Kani public-writer buffer lengths are fixed at 6 and 10; arbitrary valid slice lengths are quantified separately by the Lean theorems. The write_int documentation says nonrepresentable values panic, while original-body theorems establish truncation for all i64 inputs when count/space guards hold; 176 finite native observations corroborate selected boundaries. This is a documentation/behavior mismatch, not a memory-safety finding. write_i24 documents only short-buffer panic and is not classified as a documentation problem. Other signed reads remain unproved. Opposite-endian conversion methods and other target configurations are not covered by the identity results. Panic messages and unwinding side effects are not specified."
